Q: What is the Python &quot;with&quot; statement designed for? A: <p>I have seen a few answers to this question using <code>@contextmanager</code>, and they are correct in their design patterns, and they are also correct to explain the concept of the &quot;<code>context managers</code> (the internal mechanics of python itself)&quot; when you use the <code>with</code> statement:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>from contextlib import contextmanager

@contextmanager
def func():
    var = 'yielding string'

    try:
        yield var
    finally:
        print('Cleaning up.')
        del var

with func() as f:
    print(f)

</code></pre>
<p>But, they have miss the underlying point that makes <code>@contextmanager</code> work: the <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> methods. A class with the <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> methods is a context manager object.</p>
<p>What <code>@contextmanager</code> is doing is wrapping your generator function in a class (<code>_GeneratorContextManager</code>) that  has <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> methods.</p>
<p><strong>The Source</strong> of <code>@contextmanager</code> (<code>.\Python314\Lib\contextlib.py</code>) minus the comments and the docstrings :</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class _GeneratorContextManager(
    _GeneratorContextManagerBase,
    AbstractContextManager,
    ContextDecorator,
):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Helper for @contextmanager decorator.&quot;&quot;&quot;

    def __enter__(self):
        del self.args, self.kwds, self.func
        try:
            return next(self.gen)
        except StopIteration:
            raise RuntimeError(&quot;generator didn't yield&quot;) from None

    def __exit__(self, typ, value, traceback):
        if typ is None:
            try:
                next(self.gen)
            except StopIteration:
                return False
            else:
                try:
                    raise RuntimeError(&quot;generator didn't stop&quot;)
                finally:
                    self.gen.close()
        else:
            if value is None:
                value = typ()
            try:
                self.gen.throw(value)
            except StopIteration as exc:
                return exc is not value
            except RuntimeError as exc:
                if exc is value:
                    exc.__traceback__ = traceback
                    return False
                if (
                    isinstance(value, StopIteration)
                    and exc.__cause__ is value
                ):
                    value.__traceback__ = traceback
                    return False
                raise
            except BaseException as exc:
                if exc is not value:
                    raise
                exc.__traceback__ = traceback
                return False
            try:
                raise RuntimeError(&quot;generator didn't stop after throw()&quot;)
            finally:
                self.gen.close()


def contextmanager(func):
    @wraps(func)
    def helper(*args, **kwds):
        return _GeneratorContextManager(func, args, kwds)
    return helper


@contextmanager
def func():
    var = 'yielding string'
    try:
        yield var
    finally:
        print('Cleaning up.')
        del var


with func() as f:
    print(f)

</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>To answer the question: the <code>with</code> statement is designed to work with any class that implements the <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> methods.</p>
<p>An example is the <code>open()</code> function. It returns an <code>io.TextIOWrapper</code> class, inheriting the base <code>io.IO</code> class containing the <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> methods. When you use <code>with open(file_path) as f:</code>, the <code>__exit__</code> method ensures <code>f.close()</code> is called automatically, whether your code finishes successfully or an <strong>exception</strong> occurs.</p>
<p><strong>Exceptions</strong> are a key reason the <code>with</code> statement exists. It acts as a guaranteed safety net:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import itertools
from threading import Thread, Event
from time import sleep


class ThreadManager:
    def __init__(self):
        self.stop_event = Event()
        self.thread = Thread(target = self.worker)

    def __enter__(self):
        self.thread.start()
        return self

    def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb):
        # This is the &quot;Guarantee&quot;: This runs even if the 'with' block crashes
        print('Cleaning up the thread and exiting.')
        self.close()

        # Return False to let any exceptions propagate (crash) normally
        return False

    def worker(self):
        for i in itertools.count(1):  # infinite loop
            if self.stop_event.is_set(): break

            # each iteration prints on the same line
            print(f&quot;Thread Heartbeat: {i}&quot;, end = '\r', flush = True)
            sleep(1)

        # clear the line
        print(' ' * 30)

    def close(self):
        self.stop_event.set()
        self.thread.join()


with ThreadManager():
    sleep(3)
    raise InterruptedError('A demonstration of the &quot;__exit__&quot; method when an exception occurs.')

</code></pre>
<p>If you run a class like this without the <code>with</code> statement and a crash occurs, the <code>close()</code> method is never called. You end up with an orphaned thread that continues to run in the background even though your main script has crashed. The with statement ensures <code>__exit__</code> method runs, and cleans up the thread.</p>
